<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=internal-combustion-engine">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Energy and power</p><h1 class="enc-title">Internal combustion engine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1794">1794</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An internal combustion engine is a heat engine in which fuel burns with air or another oxidiser inside the engine, and the expanding gases push directly on pistons, turbine blades, a rotor or a nozzle; Robert Street patented one in 1794. In 1807 Nicéphore and Claude Niépce's Pyréolophore, fired by dust explosions, powered a boat on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Commercially successful engines followed in the mid-19th century, and Nicolaus Otto's four-stroke engine of 1876 was the first modern one. They are now the main power source of cars, aircraft and boats.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/internal-combustion-engine.jpg" alt="Internal combustion engine" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: VX1NG · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:18XER_engine_block.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Various scientists and engineers contributed to the engine's development. In 1791 John Barber developed a gas turbine, and Robert Street's patent of 1794 described an engine that was also the first to use liquid fuel. In 1798 John Stevens built the first American internal combustion engine. In 1807 the Swiss engineer François Isaac de Rivaz invented a hydrogen-based engine ignited by an electric spark, and in 1808 he fitted it to a primitive working vehicle. In 1823 Samuel Brown patented the first internal combustion engine applied industrially. The first such engines did not compress the mixture, and these attempts to imitate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-engine">steam engine</a> were very inefficient.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>An internal combustion engine burns fuel with an oxidiser, usually air, in a combustion chamber that is part of the engine's working fluid flow circuit, and the expanding gases act directly on parts of the engine. The term usually means engines with intermittent combustion, such as the two-stroke and four-stroke piston engines and the Wankel rotary engine. A second class uses continuous combustion: gas turbines, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jet-engine">jet engines</a> and most rocket engines. In external combustion engines, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-engine">steam engines</a>, the working fluid is kept separate from the combustion products. Engines are usually run on hydrocarbon fuels such as gasoline, diesel fuel or natural gas, and as early as 1900 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-diesel">Rudolf Diesel</a> was using peanut oil.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Most internal combustion engines serve in mobile applications and are the primary power supply of cars, aircraft and boats. Reciprocating piston engines are by far the most common power source for land and water vehicles, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/automobile">automobiles</a>, motorcycles, ships and, to a lesser extent, locomotives. The first engines were started by turning the flywheel, and the Daimler Reitwagen was started with a hand crank. All automobiles with such engines were started this way until Charles Kettering developed the electric starter.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Internal combustion engine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
